  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nGOES visible satellite shows mostly clear skies across Upper \nMichigan this afternoon as surface high pressure extends over the \narea from northern Ontario and low pressure over the Ohio River \nValley continues to pull farther away from the region. This supports \na pleasant day in dry northerly flow across the area, with \ntemperatures mainly in the 70s aside from some 60s along the Lake \nSuperior shoreline. Tonight will be one of the chillier nights of \nthe summer so far as clear skies, a dry airmass, and diminishing \nwinds support optimal radiational cooling processes and allow temps \nto drop well into the 40s across much of the interior UP. Do not \nexpect temps to be quite cold enough for consideration of any frost \nheadlines, but would certainly not be surprised to see a few \nsheltered locations dip into the upper 30s for a brief time tonight. \nLocations near the shorelines will stay a bit warmer in the low to \nmid 50s.\n\nDry weather will continue into Monday as upper level ridging builds \ninto the Great Lakes from the northern Plains, with rising 500 mb \nheights and 850 mb temps increasing to 16-18 C supporting highs \njumping back into the low to mid 80s for much of the area Monday \nafternoon. This will also allow overnight lows to return back to \nseasonal norms in the mid 50s to low 60s Monday night.\n\nThe next chance for showers and thunderstorms comes on Tuesday as a \nshortwave trough swings through northwest Ontario and drives a \nsurface cold front through the UP. This does not appear to be a slam \ndunk as the best forcing looks to remain north of the area, but can \nreasonably expect at least some shower activity during the day on \nTuesday. The best chance for a strong thunderstorm will likely be \nover the western and southcentral UP where instability looks to be \nmaximized, but overall severe potential looks to be on the low side \nas bulk shear remains marginal (30-35 kt). SPC subsequently only has \nthe UP in a general thunderstorm risk for now, but will continue to \nmonitor as the finer details come into clearer focus in the coming \ndays. Heavy rainfall concerns also appear to be rather minimal given \nthe quick hitting nature of the system, as NBM probs currently only \ndepict a 10-20% chance for a wetting rainfall of at least a quarter \ninch across the area through Tuesday night. Still, will continue to \nmonitor as PWATS briefly spike back up above 1.5\" on Tuesday.\n\nTemperatures fall back into the 70s on Wednesday behind the front, \nbut look to quickly rebound back into the 80s on Thursday as \nadditional systems bring more chances for precipitation from Friday \ninto the weekend.",
